==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|nūllus||not any}} + {{m|la|fidēs||faith}} + {{m|en|-ian}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌnʌl.əˈfɪd.i.ən/|/nul-/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-nullifidian.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[sceptic]]; an [[atheist]] or [[unbeliever]].
#* {{RQ:Eliot Middlemarch|volume=I|book=I|chapter=IV|page=56|passage=[...] Celia was no longer the eternal cherub, but a thorn in her spirit, a pink-and-white '''nullifidian''', worse than any discouraging presence in the '[[w:Pilgrim's Progress|Pilgrim's Progress]].'}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1892|author=w:George Gissing|title=w:Born in Exile|text=A man may be content to remain a '''nullifidian'''; women cannot rest at that stage. They demand the spiritual significance of everything.}}

===Adjective===
{{head|en|adjective}}

# Of no [[faith]] or [[religion]]; not trusting to faith for [[salvation]].

